            <h3 class="font-bold text-charcoal-900 text-base">How do I choose the right model?</h3>
            <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-steel-400 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform shrink-0 ml-4" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
          </summary>
          <p class="mt-3 text-steel-600 text-sm leading-relaxed">Most buyers choose one of the four core models by seed hardness: <b>HN325</b> for everyday soft seeds, <b>HN365</b> for hard seeds and all-day double-station work, <b>HN426</b> for premium cold pressing, and <b>HN500</b> for the hardest seeds at maximum force. Specialized models cover sesame, coconut milk and oil-residue dehydration.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="bg-white rounded-xl border border-steel-100 p-5 group shadow-sm">
          <summary class="flex items-center justify-between cursor-pointer list-none">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-charcoal-900 text-base">Does a higher model number mean more kg per batch?</h3>
            <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-steel-400 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform shrink-0 ml-4" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
          </summary>
          <p class="mt-3 text-steel-600 text-sm leading-relaxed"><b>No.</b> The model number indicates pressing force, not batch capacity. The HN325, HN426 and HN500 share the same 390 mm barrel at up to 100 kg per batch; the HN365 is a vertical double-station frame configured to order. To run more batches per shift, choose double-barrel configuration — not a bigger model number.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="bg-white rounded-xl border border-steel-100 p-5 group shadow-sm">
          <summary class="flex items-center justify-between cursor-pointer list-none">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-charcoal-900 text-base">Single or double barrel?</h3>
            <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-steel-400 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform shrink-0 ml-4" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
          </summary>
          <p class="mt-3 text-steel-600 text-sm leading-relaxed">Startup / testing market → single barrel (lower cost, compact). Commercial / continuous operation → double barrel (one presses, other loads, eliminates idle time between batches). Double barrel doesn't press 2 batches at once — it shares one hydraulic system between two barrels. The HN365 extends this with an alternating vertical double-station layout. <b>The configuration must be chosen when ordering — a single-barrel frame cannot be converted to double barrel later.</b></p>
        </details>
        <details class="bg-white rounded-xl border border-steel-100 p-5 group shadow-sm">
          <summary class="flex items-center justify-between cursor-pointer list-none">
